---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { SOURCES, table } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('method', lang);
const title = 'Visa Calculator Method 2026: how each result is worked out';
const description = 'How VisaCalc computes the Skilled Worker threshold, visa fees and surcharge, spouse visa income, ILR and citizenship dates from GOV.UK sources, and its limits.';
const S = K.P.skilledWorker, ST = K.P.settlement, C = K.P.citizenship, F = K.P.family;
const src = (k: string, t?: string) => `<a href="${SOURCES[k].url}" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">${t ?? SOURCES[k].label}</a>`;
const tests = [
  ['Skilled Worker, 2 years from Argentina (GOV.UK example)', K.gbp(2889), K.gbp(K.visaCost({ route: 'skilled-worker', where: 'outside', months: 24, partner: false, children: 0 }).total)],
  ['Family visa surcharge, adult, 2 years 9 months (GOV.UK)', K.gbp(3105), K.gbp(K.ihs(33, 'outside', false))],
  ['Family visa surcharge, child, 5 years (GOV.UK)', K.gbp(3880), K.gbp(K.ihs(60, 'outside', true))],
  ['Student surcharge, 2-year visa (GOV.UK)', K.gbp(1552), K.gbp(K.ihs(24, 'outside', true))],
  ['Naturalisation with ceremony (GOV.UK)', K.gbp(1839), K.gbp(K.FEE.naturalisation() + K.FEE.ceremony())],
  ['60 hours at £15, counted salary (Appendix Skilled Worker SW 14.3)', K.gbp(37440), K.gbp(K.checkSkilledWorker({ code: '1111', salary: 46800, hours: 60, situation: 'standard', nation: 'England' }).countedSalary)],
  ['Immigration skills charge, 5 years, small sponsor (GOV.UK)', K.gbp(2400), K.gbp(K.skillsCharge(60, true))],
  ['Citizenship: back on 25 June 2017, first date to apply (GOV.UK)', '24 June 2022', K.date(K.citizenshipDate('2017-01-01', '2021-06-20', [{ out: '2017-06-10', back: '2017-06-25' }], false).earliest!)],
];
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How VisaCalc calculates', description, path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'How we calculate', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'How we calculate' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>How VisaCalc calculates</h1>
    <p>Every result on this site is a published rule applied to the figures you enter. The rules come from GOV.UK only, are kept in dated parameter files, and each calculation is tested against the worked examples GOV.UK prints. Rules were last read on {K.date(K.P.retrieved_at)}; the fee table is the one in force from {K.date(K.FEE_DATE)}.</p>
    <h2>1. Fees</h2>
    <p>The Home Office publishes its fees as a spreadsheet, a new one with each change ({K.FEES.versions.length} since {K.date(K.FEES.versions[0].date)}). The site reads every version and keeps each line with its history; a calculator asks for "Skilled Worker, up to three years, outside the UK" and receives the amount of that line in the current table. If the Home Office renames a line, the lookup fails and the site is not rebuilt until it is fixed, so an old figure cannot stay online by accident. Source: <Fragment set:html={src('fees_table')} />.</p>
    <h2>2. Immigration health surcharge</h2>
    <p>{K.gbp(K.P.ihs.standard)} per person per year, {K.gbp(K.P.ihs.reduced)} for students, their dependants, Youth Mobility applicants and anyone under 18 when applying. Six months or less: nothing from outside the UK, half a year from inside. Longer: whole years, plus half a year if the remainder is six months or less, or a full year if more. Not charged on Health and Care Worker visas, visitor visas or settlement. Source: <Fragment set:html={src('ihs')} />.</p>
    <h2>3. Skilled Worker salary</h2>
    <p>The tool reads the {K.int(K.OCCUPATIONS.length)} occupation codes of Appendix Skilled Occupations with their table, going rates and printed discounted rates, the Immigration Salary List with its nations and criteria, and the Temporary Shortage List of SW 6.1A. For your code it decides whether the code is open (Table 1, Table 3, or Table 1a through a list or the transitional rules), takes the going rate, multiplies it by your weekly hours over {S.goingRateHours}, counts at most {S.maxCountedHours} hours of your salary, and requires the higher of the floor and the rate of your option. Health and education codes use the pay band you choose (Agenda for Change, teachers' scales or doctors' grades) with a {K.gbp(S.healthEducation.floor)} floor. Sources: <Fragment set:html={src('appendix_sw')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('appendix_aso')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('appendix_isl')} />.</p>
    <Fragment set:html={table(['Option', 'Who', 'Floor', 'Rate'], [['A', 'Standard', K.gbp(S.general), 'going rate'], ['B', 'PhD relevant to the job', K.gbp(S.phdOther.floor), K.pct(S.phdOther.share)], ['C', 'STEM PhD', K.gbp(S.phdStem.floor), K.pct(S.phdStem.share)], ['D', 'Immigration Salary List', K.gbp(S.isl.floor), 'going rate'], ['E', 'New entrant, postdoc', K.gbp(S.newEntrant.floor), K.pct(S.newEntrant.share)], ['F to J', 'Sponsored before 4 April 2024', `${K.gbp(S.transitional.newEntrant)} to ${K.gbp(S.transitional.general)}`, 'Table 2 rates'], ['K', 'Listed health or education job', K.gbp(S.healthEducation.floor), 'pay scale']], 'Points options of Appendix Skilled Worker (table following SW 4.4).', ['l', 'l', 'r', 'r'])} />
    <h2>4. Spouse and partner income</h2>
    <p>Required income: {K.gbp(F.minimumIncome)}; for partners first granted before 11 April 2024 who extend, {K.gbp(F.legacy.base)} plus {K.gbp(F.legacy.firstChild)} for the first child and {K.gbp(F.legacy.otherChild)} for each other non-British child, capped at {K.gbp(F.legacy.cap)}. Savings needed to cover a shortfall: {K.gbp(F.savingsBase)} plus {F.savingsMultiplier} times the shortfall ({F.savingsMultiplierIlr} time at settlement). Sources: <Fragment set:html={src('family_income')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('appendix_fm')} />.</p>
    <h2>5. ILR date and absences</h2>
    <p>Qualifying years counted from the start date you enter; application possible {ST.applyEarlyDays} days before they end (Appendix Continuous Residence, CR 1.1). Absences: whole days only, so a trip from 1 July to 10 August counts 39 days; at most {ST.maxAbsenceDays} days in any 12-month window, each window ending on a day abroad and starting the day after the same date a year earlier. A breach restarts the period on the return date of the trip that caused it. Long residence keeps the old limits of {ST.longResidenceLegacy.totalDays} days in total and {ST.longResidenceLegacy.singleDays} at a time for absences that began before 11 April 2024. Sources: <Fragment set:html={src('continuous_residence')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('cr_guidance')} />.</p>
    <h2>6. Citizenship date</h2>
    <p>The first day, on or after twelve months of ILR (or the ILR date for the spouse of a British citizen), when: the five years before it (three for a spouse) hold no more than {C.maxAbsence5y} days abroad ({C.spouseMaxAbsence3y}), the last twelve months no more than {C.maxAbsence12m}, and you were in the UK on the first day of the period. Sources: <Fragment set:html={src('citizenship')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('citizenship_spouse')} />.</p>
    <h2>7. Decision dates</h2>
    <p>The UKVI service standards are in weeks of the UK working week and include public holidays, so a standard of three weeks is counted as 21 calendar days from the identity check or biometrics. Priority (five working days) and super priority (next working day, two for settlement) skip weekends and bank holidays of England and Wales. Sources: <Fragment set:html={src('times_outside')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('times_inside')} />.</p>
    <h2>8. Tests against GOV.UK examples</h2>
    <Fragment set:html={table(['Case', 'GOV.UK or the rules', 'This site'], tests, 'Each case is also an automated test that must pass before the site is published.', ['l', 'r', 'r'])} />
    <p>One discrepancy found on GOV.UK itself: the Graduate visa page gives £1,152.50 as the surcharge for an 18-month visa, while the surcharge rules give {K.gbp(K.ihs(18, 'inside', false))} (one year plus half a year at {K.gbp(K.P.ihs.standard)}). The calculators follow the surcharge rules; the Home Office computes the exact amount when you apply.</p>
    <h2>9. Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The tools do not judge evidence, genuineness, suitability (criminal record, past breaches) or the caseworker's discretion.</li>
      <li>The occupation code must be the one your sponsor would choose; a wrong code changes everything.</li>
      <li>Visa lengths are entered in months; the Home Office calculates the surcharge from the exact dates, which can add half a year when a visa runs a few days past a whole year.</li>
      <li>Rules that have been proposed but not laid (earned settlement) are not applied.</li>
      <li>Not legal advice; not affiliated with the Home Office.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
